hi this is presto occur consider a floor  with horizontal lines that are spaced  two units apart you drop a needle of  length one on the floor sometimes when  you drop the needle it'll intersect one  of the horizontal lines but other times  it won't you can imagine repeating this  experiment many times the question for  you is Buffon x' needle problem what is  the probability that a randomly dropped  needle will cross one of the horizontal  lines if you're adventurous give this  problem a try  otherwise keep watching the video I'm  going to present two different proofs of  this problem one way to solve this  problem involves trigonometry and  calculus we're going to consider all the  different ways that this needle of  length one could fall on the floor it  can fall in different vertical positions  but it could also fall at different  angles in this method we're going to  consider all the different vertical  positions and angles and compute the  proportion at which the needle  intersects the horizontal line will  magnify our diagram and we're going to  focus on the middle point of the needle  as a reference this is at 1/2 distance  from one of the ends one of the  variables we'll define is the distance D  which is the distance of the middle part  of the needle to the closest horizontal  line D will be between 0 &amp; 1 this is  because the middle part of the needle  can at most be halfway from one of the  horizontal lines the next variable we'll  define is the angle theta we'll define  this as the acute angle of the needle  relative to the horizontal lines data  will always be between 0 and PI over 2  or 0 and 90 degrees now for a given  distance of the middle part of the  needle to the horizontal line  when will the needle intersect one of  the horizontal lines well it will depend  on the angle that the needle makes you  could think about for a given distance  of the middle part of the needle the  locus of points that the needle could  spin around as an angle  now some of these points the needle will  intersect the horizontal line but some  of them the needle will not so at which  angles will the needle intersect the  horizontal line will use a little bit of  trigonometry we can consider the leg  which is the vertical part of the needle  we know the hypotenuse of the needle is  1/2 because we have half the needle we  can compute this leg of the right  triangle as equaling 1/2 sine of theta  so now when will the needle actually  intersect the horizontal line well this  is when the vertical distance is larger  than the variable D in other word the  needle will cross the horizontal line if  and only if 1/2 sine of theta is greater  than or equal to D well now consider the  space of D and theta and make a graph  the condition that the needle crosses is  the shaded region in this graph so we  can now compute the probability the  needle crosses a horizontal line as  equaling the area under the curve  divided by the area of the entire sample  space which is the rectangle the area  under the curve we can compute using  calculus we'll calculate the integral  from 0 to PI over 2 of 1/2 sine of theta  D theta and that gives us the result of  1/2 the area of the rectangle is the  Eldar part of the equation  while the width is 1/2 and the height is  1 so that means the area of the  rectangle is PI over 2 and that gives us  the probability the needle will  intersect one of the horizontal lines as  1 divided by pi and so this kind of fun  the constant pi appears in this problem  in fact this gives us a numerical way a  practical way that we could actually  approximate pi if you drop the needle on  a floor which has horizontal lines that  were double the size of the needle you  can repeat the experiment n times and  count the number of intersections or  successes X for what we just computed X  divided by n will approach 1 divided by  pi and so the reciprocal if you take the  total number of trials and divide  by the numbers successes that'll give  you an approximation for pi you can give  it a try you might need hundreds or  thousands of tosses to get any sort of  accuracy but it is a physical way that  you could approximate pi so now I'm  going to continue to the second proof  which is Barbieri's proof of Buffon  zeidel we start out with our diagram and  our needle of length 1 but in order to  get to this Bart this proof we're going  to no need to know something about  probability so I'm going to make it a  lot more complicated but this is  actually going to stay with it it's  actually going to get us to our solution  that's going to appear like magic now  for any needle the expected number of  intersections will be 1 times the  probability of having one intersection  plus 2 times the probability of having  two intersections plus three times the  probability of having three  intersections and so on this is the  definition for the expected value of a  discrete random variable so here we have  P of K being the probability that a  needle intersects K times and we're  multiplying it by K now when you have a  needle of length one this acquit this  expression is going to simplify greatly  for a needle length one you can at most  have one intersection the number of  times it can intersect two or three or  so on all of these are going to be equal  to zero there's of course the pedantic  point that you could have the needle  fall exactly horizontally on one of the  lines that would be an infinite number  of intersections but this happens with  measure zero so we can ignore that  extreme possibility if we define e of L  as the expected number of intersections  for a straight needle of length L we've  just figured out that a of one is equal  to P one in other words the expected  number of intersections for straight  needle of length 1 is equal to exactly  the probability you want the probability  that the needle will intersect one of  the horizontal lines but now that we've  used  we made this complicated but now we've  actually reframed the problem in terms  of expectations and if you and this is  where things get interesting so imagine  we subdivide this needle of length 1  in two segments of x and y so you can  think about this as two different  needles of length x and y that have been  joined together what's the total number  of intersections for this needle well by  the linearity of the expectation  operator it's equal to the expected  numbers of just that segment X plus just  this segment Y now these two are not  independent events but nonetheless the  expectation operator is linear and this  is going to give us something  interesting we can actually imagine  needles a different shape imagine a bent  needle that has one line segment X and  another line segment Y this equation is  still going to be true even for bent  needles or needles of different shapes  so now we're going to use if you were  going to create some results about  expectations if you have a needle of  length n the expected number of  intersections of that will be n times  the expected number of intersections of  a needle of length just one in fact we  can generalize this if you have a needle  of length x say a plig '''l needle which  has a perimeter of X the expected number  of intersections will be x times the  expected number of intersections for a  needle of length 1 and to be pedantic  this is where x is a rational number so  now we're going to take all this and now  we're going to drop a very special  needle we'll return to our grid and now  we're going to drop a needle which is a  circle of radius 1 if that needle  happens to fall here it intersects  exactly two times if it drops here you  get one intersection at the top and one  at the bottom and no matter how you drop  this circular needle of radius 1 it's  going to intersect exactly two times now  we're going to relate it to our results  about dropping needles Apple ignant  lengths so imagine we have an inscribed  polygon with n sides and it's  circumscribed polygon events I  I've pictured hexagons but you can  imagine n sided polygons that inscribe  and circumscribed the circle the  expected number of intersections on the  circle can be bounded by the inscribed  and circumscribed polygons and we know  the expected number of intersections for  the circle is always 2 what can we say  about the expected number of  intersections of our polygonal needles  from our results before the expected  number of intersections is related to  the lengths of these polygons so we have  the perimeters of the polygons times the  expected number of intersections for a  needle of length 1 now we do something  interesting we let the number of sides  of these polygons go to infinity as that  happens the inscribed and circumscribed  polygons will converge to the circle  which means that their perimeters are  going to converge to the circumference  of the circle which is equal to 2 pi and  we can substitute in 2 pi for those  perimeters and now we proven that the  expected value of 1 is equal to 2  divided by 2 over pi in other words the  expected number of intersections for  needle length 1 or the probability the  needle intersects one of the lines is  equal to 1 divided by PI and that's the  clever and unique way to figure out who  founds needle problem thanks for  watching this video please subscribe to  my channel I make videos on math and  game theory you can catch me on my blog  mind your decisions which you can follow  on Facebook Google+ and patreon you can  catch me on social media at pressure  Locker and if you liked this video  please check out my books they're linked  in the video description
 

